						<TD><IMG src="images/blank.gif" width="10"></TD>
						<TD width="100%"><TABLE width="100%" border="0" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" style="font-family:verdana;font-size:8pt">
							<TR><TD>
								<TABLE width="100%" cellspacing="10">
  <TR><TD colspan=2 valign="top">
    Loot is a two-edged sword. We all want it and it is fun to get, but it has the potential to create fights
    because inevitably a decision must be made as to who gets it and who doesnt. Unfortunately, sometimes the person who
    doesnt get it gets bitter, and that basically just sucks for everyone. There is no way we can change how you feel if
    you feel robbed by loot decisions, but just to clarify how and why we do loot the way we do, the HC wanted to set out
    a few principles.
  </TD></TR>
  <TR><TD valign="top">1.</TD><TD valign="top">
    When it comes to loot, we recognize that everyone legitimately wants it. It's ok to want to loot up your
    character. BUT, remember that we are a guild and when your number doesnt come up on a loot decision, please try to
    feel happy for the folks whose number did come up instead of getting bitter. In an ideal world we would all be
    arguing that the other guy should get an item rather than arguing you got robbed.
  </TD></TR>
  <TR><TD valign="top">2.</TD><TD valign="top">
	The HC makes loot decisions. Why is this? First of all, /random is almost always an injustice. There are
	reasons for making loot decisions that are better than "I was there when it dropped." These include, supporting
	the guild, attendence, seniority, need, what you have gotten from the guild in the past, etc. Sure, we make
	mistakes, but we feel a system that tries to make an informed and fair decision rather than a /random is more
	fair and better for the continuing future of the guild. We try to reserve /random for situation in which there
	is no good way to break a tie. We hope you all trust we are trying to be fair and act for the good of the guild
	as a whole. If you think we are unfair and arbitrary jerks, then there is really no reason for you to be in the
	guild anyway.
  </TD></TR>
  <TR><TD valign="top">3.</TD><TD valign="top">
	Rarely, the HC may decide to reserve loot for someone who was not on a particular raid. As a general rule,
	attendance at a raid where a piece of loot drops is a very large factor in making a loot decision, but this
	simply isnt always fair. Take, for example, dragon raids. Some folks have done very well on loot in just
	attending a few raids. Others attended many raids and got nothing (which is just a function of the loot table).
	Still others have been great supporters of the guild, but were over the level limit before the raids even started.
	Others had great attendance in the past, but now cant go because of the level restriction. So, in a given
	situation if a piece of good loot dropped and the only person that could use it was someone who had only been
	one or two times or someone who already has gotten some dragon loot, the loot might be awarded to someone who
	had excellent attendance in the past over the new participant.
  </TD></TR>
  <TR><TD valign="top">4.</TD><TD valign="top">
	LSoG Loot is not Rented. If it was awarded to you it is yours. However, we would PREFER you treat loot as a
	guild asset. People have already done this. For example, Kiien and Sorefist passed down the belts they were
	wearing when they got RBBS on dragon raids. Follow this example. When you replace a piece of gear that you got
	through a guild raid, it would be preferred that you used it to continue to help the guild rather than auctioning
	it off.
  </TD></TR>
  <TR><TD valign="top">5.</TD><TD valign="top">
	Dont quit showing up to raids after you are all looted up. I dont think we have a huge problem with this, but
	the system only works if you help the folks that helped you.
  </TD></TR>
  <TR><TD valign="top">6.</TD><TD valign="top">
	Complaints -- If you want to talk with an officer about loot decisions, you should contact one in tell
	and hash it out. Having these fights in /guildsay or any other public channel just makes everyone feel like hell.
  </TD></TR>
  <TR><TD valign="top">7.</TD><TD valign="top">
	Sellables are collected by any of the officers. Sellable is an item wich is only good to sell to a vendor.
	The money earned thru selling those items will be put into the guild funds. Those funds are used to pay the
	needed supplies for a raid. It also can happend that the money is used to do a good trade wich helps the guild.
	in any way. If you have spent your own money for raids you can also contact the officers and ask for a repay of
	the used money.
  </TD></TR>
</TABLE>

